          "question": "I am 34 weeks pregnant with swelling in my hands and a headache that will not shift. How urgent is this, and what should I be asking about?",
          "answer": "**Please contact your maternity or obstetric team today.** Don't wait for a routine appointment. The automated check labelled this routine, but that label does not match the guidance I found. The headache-guidance summary (red-flag-sudden-severe-headache) lists a new headache in pregnancy, \"particularly with visual changes, swelling or upper abdominal pain,\" under **same-day medical assessment**. You have a new headache that won't go away, plus swelling, at 34 weeks.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if you have any of these: sudden vision changes (blurring, spots, or loss of sight), pain in the upper abdomen, confusion or drowsiness, a seizure, or a headache that suddenly becomes much worse.\n\nQuestions to ask when you call or are seen:\n- Does this combination of headache and swelling need checking today?\n- Should my blood pressure be checked?\n- Which warning signs should make me go to hospital straight away, rather than phone first?\n- Do I need any tests today?\n\nThe sources I searched do not say what is causing your symptoms, and they do not cover pregnancy blood pressure checks or treatment. Those questions are for your clinician. This is educational information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
